. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H4 * 
. 
.         * Please add a verbal description of your model here
.         
.         /* 
>         
> We estimate the effect of having a daughter compared to having a son as a fir
> st child on gender norms by gender of the parent. Yi represents our outcome v
> ariable. d_daughter represents the indicator variable for having a daughter a
> s a first child. father represents the indicator variable for being a father.
>  Our coefficient of interest is estimated by 1. Xi, represents a set of con
> trol variables: To account for the fact that having any additional child or a
>  daughter as a subsequent child might also affect gender norms, we control fo
> r the number of brothers and for the number of sisters of the first-born chil
> d at the time of the survey.
> Our approach relies on the assumption that the sex of the first child is exog
> enous to parents. The assumption would be violated if parents had an influenc
> e on the sex of their first born, for example through selective abortion of u
> nborn girls or boys. §15 of the Genetic Diagnostics Act is designed to preven
> t parents from aborting an unborn child based on undesired sex. Until the 14t
> h week of pregnancy, abortions are generally illegal under criminal law, but 
> they are not prosecuted and remain exempt from punishment under certain condi
> tions. After the 14th week of pregnancy has passed, abortions are only permit
> ted if the pregnant woman faces a life-threatening situation or the risk of a
>  serious impairment to her physical or mental health. We therefore think that
>  our assumption holds for the German context.
> We take the family as the unit that is treated and hence based on Abadie et a
> l. (2022)cluster standard errors at the household level.
>         
>         */
.         
.         reghdfe plh0308_v2 d_daughter##father n_brothers n_sisters fchild_age
> , absorb (syear) cluster(hid) coeflegend
